g to handle long flags, add order checking logic to the comparison and drain functions, make the delimiter configurable in the write function, and add test cases. For the order checking behavior, I'm looking at how GNU comm handles it—by default it warns on the first violation per file and exits with code 1, the `--check-order` flag makes it exit immediately on violation, and `--nocheck-order` disables warnings entirely. The warning message format is \"comm: file N is not in sorted order\".\n\nI'm realizing there's a discrepancy between what the spec says and what GNU comm actually does—the spec suggests default mode continues processing while `--check-order` stops immediately, but my testing shows both exit with code 1. I need to verify the actual behavior more carefully to understand whether default mode truly continues to completion before exiting or if it also stops early.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2046,"cache_read_input_tokens":30962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2046},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92d765ad-c907-4b42-ab21-284ff239170c","uuid":"9ae99a3a-8eb5-461b-a2f3-8b7155004f51"}
